{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqq1rh9jnxp","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Stale-claim reaper at 10min breaks judge-readiness propagation for long impl workers","summary":"OBSERVED (2026-06-22, feature bench-prod-parity, N=2): an impl worker running >10min gets its claim auto-released by the stale-claim reaper (\"auto-released: worker 'X' not running (stale >10m)\"), and the subsequent late `tested` completion does NOT propagate readiness to the blocking judge task — the judge stays not_ready (P1: 13.5min run → judge stuck not_ready). A worker finishing <10min completes cleanly and its judge auto-readies on `tested` (P2: 5min run → judge went ready). So the 10-min reaper threshold is too aggressive for multi-minute impl workers, AND the late-completion transition skips dependent-ready propagation (likely because the claim lease is already gone when `complete` fires, so the transition handler takes a path that doesn't re-derive dependents). SEPARATE GOTCHA confirmed same session: raw merge_attempt does the git merge ONLY — the impl must additionally be set_status done to release its impl-dependents; the judge's submit_verdict bundles merge+done, the raw escape hatch does not. FIX DIRECTION (candidate task P7): (a) raise/disable the stale threshold while a worker is actively progressing (heartbeat or longer TTL), and (b) re-run dependent-readiness derivation on the `tested` transition regardless of claim-lease state.","category":null,"tags":["orchestrator","reaper","judge-readiness","feature:bench-prod-parity"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-23T02:50:33.127Z","vec":[-0.07544713467359543,-0.03820882365107536,0.03849656879901886,-0.01908394880592823,0.06383257359266281,-0.03510674089193344,-0.02356717921793461,0.01633026823401451,0.021805694326758385,0.060147013515233994,0.06164832413196564,-0.020454438403248787,-0.04802988842129707,0.0012671208241954446,0.017855456098914146,0.023540059104561806,0.09992628544569016,-0.05284014344215393,-0.056884422898292